(function(){window.FP_LIB=window.FP_LIB||{reports:[]};var seen=new Set(window.FP_LIB.reports.map(function(r){return r.id;}));[{"id":"A139","date":"2026-11-03","cat":"Selección y valoración","title":"VFC — 3 noviembre: pendiente por normalización; sin DCF","text":"PUBLICACIÓN DE INVESTIGACIÓN · 3 NOVIEMBRE 2026\nVFC: PENDIENTE · BLOQUEADA | Calidad: not-decision-ready | Decisión: BLOCKED_PENDING_ESSENTIAL_NORMALIZATION\nFecha del análisis: 2026-11-03. Precio de referencia: cierre regular del 2026-11-02, $19.39 USD.\nRecovery watch. Retained-business tariff timing and the Dickies cash-flow carve-out remain essential evidence gaps. No DCF, certified fair value or new entry is approved.\nSin nuevas compras autorizadas. Precio máximo de compra certificado: ninguno. Stop aprobado: ninguno. Acciones autorizadas: 0. La publicación no activa Stage 3.5.\n\n# VFC — recovery watch, essential normalization blocked\n\n**Research date:** November 3, 2026. **Price reference:** November 2 regular close, **$19.39**. Latest earnings were October 28, outside the user's excluded November 2–6 earnings week.\n\n## Decision\n\n**No approved entry. Do not commission the full DCF yet.** VF shows genuine recovery evidence, but the retained-business tariff timing and cash-flow bridge are not sufficiently complete to certify sustainable TTM growth. This is an evidence blocker, **not a conclusion that the business has failed**. No fair value, target, margin of safety, reverse DCF or entry range is presented.\n\nThe North Face and Timberland remain core businesses; **Timberland was not sold**. Dickies was sold November 12, 2025, but remains in historical GAAP continuing operations. Supreme was sold October 1, 2024 and is excluded from the continuing-operation histories below. Confusing these perimeters materially distorts recovery.\n\n## What improved—and what has not been proved\n\nThe exact Dickies recast was recovered from the issuer's November 2025 supplemental filing. Management-adjusted retained EBIT is:\n\n- FY2025: **$529.419 million**; FY2026: **$649.974 million**.\n- H1 FY2025: **$194.966 million**; H1 FY2026: **$255.108 million**; H1 FY2027: **$269.168 million**.\n- Current TTM: **$664.034 million**; prior TTM: **$589.561 million**; mechanical growth **12.63%**.\n\nThese are management-adjusted figures, **not yet economically normalized earnings**. H1 retained sales growth includes currency; organic growth is modest. The North Face/Timberland improvement coexists with continuing Vans weakness. EPS growth also benefits from lower interest and tax, rather than solely operating improvement.\n\n### The load-bearing tariff mismatch\n\nFY2026 recorded a $149.7 million refund receivable, with $93.8 million reducing COGS for goods already sold and $55.9 million offsetting tariffs in inventory. The inventory offset subsequently passed through COGS as goods sold in H1 FY2027. It is incorrect to remove that $55.9 million without also removing the matched tariff inventory cost.\n\nHowever, the FY2026 Q4 catch-up also reverses some earlier expenses. Let **x** be retained-business H1 FY2026 tariff expense later refunded. The tariff-neutral rolling comparison becomes:\n\n**Current EBIT = 664.034 − x; prior EBIT = 589.561 + x.**\n\n| Illustrative x, $M | Corrected growth |\n|---:|---:|\n| 0 | 12.63% |\n| 20 | 5.66% |\n| 30 | 2.34% |\n| 37.2365 | 0.00% |\n| 50 | −3.99% |\n\nThe prior 10-Q says margin impacts were expected mainly in the second half, which supports a smaller first-half effect qualitatively, but does not quantify x or establish a reliable bound. The 5% reference is **not** a mandatory plan cutoff: 0–5% may qualify with a greater discount; negative growth requires demonstrated recovery. The unresolved input changes that classification, so it cannot be silently assumed zero.\n\n## Three-year financial review\n\nUSD millions. GAAP continuing operations exclude Supreme but include Dickies until sale; **not a fully retained-business cash series**.\n\n| Fiscal year | Revenue | GAAP EBIT | Continuing net income | CFO | PPE + software | Strict FCF | SBC |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 2024 | 9,915.678 | −143.935 | −1,018.477 | 884.714 | 197.245 | 687.469 | 57.829 |\n| 2025 | 9,504.691 | 303.773 | 69.324 | 438.489 | 126.023 | 312.466 | 73.247 |\n| 2026 | 9,605.207 | 576.569 | 254.920 | 671.274 | 165.806 | 505.468 | 76.954 |\n\nFY2024's tax receivable write-off, impairments and working-capital release make its loss and cash nonrepresentative. FY2026 includes a net pension-termination cash benefit of approximately $100 million; the company's normalized FCF comparator is approximately $405 million. The Dickies sale generated cash and a $127.2 million gain below EBIT, not recurring earnings. A FY2024 adjusted ex-Dickies EBIT recast was not found and is explicitly missing.\n\n## Four recent quarters\n\nCash FCF includes PPE and capitalized software. Quarter cash amounts are derived from cumulative statements when necessary; no seasonal quarter is annualized.\n\n| Quarter | Retained revenue | Retained management-adjusted EBIT | Reported continuing FCF |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Q3 FY2026 | 2,820.054 | 340.997 | 966.547 |\n| Q4 FY2026 | 2,166.034 | 53.870 | −8.017 |\n| Q1 FY2027 | 1,669.379 | −94.548 | −115.762 |\n| Q2 FY2027 | 2,695.132 | 363.716 | −206.889 |\n\nDisplayed issuer quarter versus half-year recasts differ by $0.001 million; the annual-plus-half-year bridge controls TTM. Seasonal cash concentration is extreme.\n\n## Cash normalization must be symmetric\n\nReported continuing TTM FCF is **$635.879 million versus $232.907 million**. TTM SBC is **$78.883 million versus $80.946 million**; subtracting it as an ownership-cost diagnostic yields $556.996 million versus $151.961 million, **still not sustainable retained-business FCFF**.\n\nIt would be misleading to subtract current tariff refunds while leaving the earlier refunded tariff payments untouched. Let **p** be tariff cash paid in H1 FY2026, distinct from expense x. Under the simplified assumptions that all $149.7 million was refunded in the current TTM and that the $100 million pension benefit is removed:\n\n**Current tariff-neutral FCF = 535.879 − p; prior = 232.907 + p.**\n\nAcross p from zero to $149.7 million, this illustrative TTM cash improvement remains positive. That does **not** certify sustainable cash: the issuer says “substantially all” refunds were received; $37.6 million vendor/partner reimbursements were payable; Dickies CFO, capex, software and working capital are not separately recast. Exact cash timing and the retained perimeter remain essential gaps. No underlying cash deterioration is asserted from a one-sided refund subtraction.\n\n## Ownership costs, debt and liquidity\n\nSBC remains an economic cost. Recurring software and lease cash also remain. Company-adjusted Reinvent exclusions require scrutiny: H1 FY2027 removes $15.680 million of gains, while prior H1 excluded $46.265 million costs. Stock-price-contingent consulting fees measured through June 2027 are not automatically free or permanently finished.\n\nSeptember carrying financial debt totals **$3,538.589 million**, with **$442.951 million unrestricted cash**; carrying net debt is $3,095.638 million. This is **not a completed principal-value bridge**. A $500 million maturity falls in April 2027. Operating lease liabilities total $1,462.225 million and H1 lease cost is $211.880 million; do not capitalize leases in EV while also double charging their expense.\n\nThe $1.5 billion asset-based facility had approximately **$1.47 billion availability**, borrowing-base dependent. Reported covenant compliance and liquidity do not imply investment-grade credit: disclosed ratings were BB/Ba2. Supplier-finance eligible payables are another working-capital consideration. No immediate financing failure is concluded, but leverage materially reduces valuation tolerance.\n\n## Outlook and what would reopen the case\n\nManagement reiterated FY2027 approximately 8% adjusted operating margin and normalized FCF flat/up versus approximately $405 million. Reported FCF of at least $515 million is a different measure; do not capitalize refund cash as perpetuity. FY2027 has a 53rd week. The longer-term 10% exit-margin ambition is a management target, not an achieved base case.\n\nBefore full Stage 3 valuation, obtain:\n1. Retained-business quarterly tariff-in-COGS, cash payments, refunds and inventory reserve bridge.\n2. Dickies cash-flow/working-capital carve-out and stranded-cost treatment for both TTMs.\n3. Economically recurring restructuring/consulting and compensation schedule.\n\nOnly then build ten-year FCFF, same-model reverse, peers and scenarios. Stage 3.5 remains inactive. Technical recovery cannot cure missing fundamental evidence.\n\n## Audit trail\n\n`research_record.json` contains the complete gated record; `gate_model.py` reproduces `calculations.json` and the annual/quarter CSVs. Primary documents and table extracts are retained. `sources.json` maps source IDs and URLs. Independent source/math QA is recorded separately.\n\nPrimary sources: [FY2026 10-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/103379/000010337926000030/vfc-20260328.htm), [latest 10-Q](https://www.vfc.com/investors/financial-information/sec-filings/content/0000103379-26-000135/vfc-20260926.htm), [Dickies recast](https://www.vfc.com/investors/financial-information/sec-filings/content/0000103379-25-000066/vfcex992supplementalfininf.htm), [prior H1 10-Q](https://www.vfc.com/investors/financial-information/sec-filings/content/0000103379-25-000060/vfc-20250927.htm), [latest results](https://www.vfc.com/investors/news-events-presentations/press-releases/detail/1872/vf-corporation-exceeds-guidance-in-second-quarter-fiscal), [November 2 close](https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/vfc/history/).\n"},{"id":"A140","date":"2026-11-03","cat":"Selección y valoración","title":"CNX — 3 noviembre: análisis completo; ESPERAR a $37.20","text":"PUBLICACIÓN DE INVESTIGACIÓN · 3 NOVIEMBRE 2026\nCNX: ESPERAR · WAIT_PRICE | Calidad: screen-grade | Decisión: WAIT_PRICE\nFecha del análisis: 2026-11-03. Precio de referencia: cierre regular del 2026-11-02, $37.20 USD.\nCurrent-price WAIT decision complete at $37.20. The conditional base value is $21.05458 per share. Commodity sensitivity is material: the favorable scenario is $68.37 and the adverse scenario is a $0 common-equity distress floor, not a liquidation value.\nSin nuevas compras autorizadas. Precio máximo de compra certificado: ninguno. Stop aprobado: ninguno. Acciones autorizadas: 0. La publicación no activa Stage 3.5.\n\n# CNX Resources — Stage 3 investment review\n**Research completed November 3, 2026 using information available through November 2 and the November 2 regular close of $37.20. USD millions unless indicated.**\n\n## Decision\n**WAIT — no approved new entry.** The fundamental gate warrants valuation work, rather than an automatic rejection on one weak quarter. Contracted 2027 realizations support a near-term recovery. However, the completed operating-driver FCFF assessment does not demonstrate a margin of safety at $37.20. The valuation model is **screen-grade**; independent source/history and model QA passed. This publication records research only. No trade, stop or portfolio allocation is authorized by this report.\n\nThe illustrative central value is **$21.05/share**. Central-deck WACC sensitivities of 9%–11% produce roughly $17–26; capital sensitivities are shown separately below. These are conditional model outputs, not a certified confidence interval. A stronger gas-price case can support substantially more than the current price. Therefore the conclusion is **insufficient demonstrated safety margin**, not certain overvaluation.\n\n## Why the business is worth researching\nCNX combines Appalachian shale and coalbed-methane production with owned midstream infrastructure and environmental-attribute activities. Hedging reduces near-term cash volatility. It is not a pure unhedged gas-price bet, but protection expires and reinvestment is necessary to offset depletion. Management’s maintenance-of-production strategy, positive recurring cash generation and a better 2027 contracted price justify completing valuation despite weak recent cash comparisons. Buybacks are capital allocation, not evidence by themselves that the stock is cheap.\n\n## Historical review: three complete years, four quarters and TTM\nThe following GAAP figures include derivative marks; the normalized bridge below is essential. EPS denominators changed materially following convertible-note settlement.\n\n| Period | Revenue | GAAP EBIT | Net income | CFO | Cash capex |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| FY2023 | 3,434.9 | 2,242.8 | 1,720.7 | 814.6 | 679.4 |\n| FY2024 | 1,266.8 | 6.4 | -90.5 | 815.8 | 540.3 |\n| FY2025 | 2,239.1 | 890.9 | 633.2 | 1,029.0 | 495.0 |\n| Q42025 | 610.5 | 269.1 | 196.3 | 297.0 | 174.4 |\n| Q12026 | 786.7 | 475.0 | 348.1 | 277.5 | 169.9 |\n| Q22026 | 618.5 | 277.8 | 202.9 | 279.5 | 142.0 |\n| Q32026 | 580.0 | 259.2 | 165.2 | 253.5 | 139.2 |\n| TTM2025 | 1,765.2 | 435.3 | 292.3 | 1,000.7 | 426.1 |\n| TTM2026 | 2,595.6 | 1,281.1 | 912.6 | 1,107.6 | 625.5 |\n\nReported diluted EPS: FY2023 $8.99, FY2024 $(0.60), FY2025 $3.98; Q4 2025 $1.28, Q1 2026 $2.18, Q2 $1.32 and Q3 $1.12. Neither the 2023 derivative-driven profit nor the 2024 loss is a sustainable earnings anchor. The multi-year cash history is much less volatile than GAAP net income. Annual-plus-YTD calculations govern TTM; minor $1,000 differences from separately printed quarters are documented in QA.\n\n### Sustainable operating profit and cash\n- Removing unrealized derivative gains, while retaining settled hedges, SBC, depletion and recurring operating costs, gives **TTM operating EBIT $686.181m versus $592.812m, up 15.8%**. This is not organic, constant-price growth. Asset-sale gains are below operating profit and are not added to normalized EBIT.\n- At a constant physical-price deck of gas $3/Mcf, NGLs $25/bbl and oil $60/bbl, TTM EBIT is approximately **$815.413m versus $815.078m: essentially flat**. Cash operating surplus after SBC slips about 0.5%. Other tested commodity decks also produce approximately flat underlying earnings.\n- Q1 2026 had a net $9m credit for unused transportation/processing capacity versus $4m prior-year expense, with colder-weather optimization contributing. Removing the entire $13m comparative benefit as a conservative sensitivity makes constant-deck earnings decline about 1.6%. That is a sensitivity, not a proven isolated nonrecurring charge or an automatic gate failure.\n- Owner cash calculated as CFO less capital investment and economic SBC, plus net equity-affiliate investment cash flow but **excluding asset-sale proceeds**, falls to **$460.432m from $546.613m**. Capex rises to $625.472m from $426.069m. Higher investment, tax timing and hedge settlement timing must be examined before calling this operational deterioration.\n- The $30m July 2026 sale of credits relating to 2025 remediation is not recurring current-year operating cash. Removing that receipt alone gives **$430.432m**, versus $546.613m. Removing aggregate working-capital sources instead gives **$429.858m versus $563.517m**. **Do not subtract both mechanically:** the credit collection may already be within the working-capital movements. The joint $399.858m result is preserved only as a conservative stress, not certified normalized cash.\n- Derivative cash timing also matters: nine-month realized hedge losses were $123.710m, while actual cash settlements paid were $208.527m; the $84.817m timing drag compares with $39.265m a year earlier. Exact Note 12 reconciliations are in independent QA. Negative cash growth cannot be attributed entirely to economic deterioration.\n\n### Gate conclusion: conditional pass to valuation, not purchase approval\nThe constant-price business is flat, but the earnings threshold is not a rigid growth-only screen. The relevant recovery evidence is **contractual**: 2027 fully covered gas volume is 409.4 Bcf at $3.30/Mcf, versus 447.2 Bcf at $2.73 in 2026. The company’s October 14 strip illustration projects a $204.99m 2027 realized hedge gain versus a $106.09m 2026 loss. These are scenario-dependent settlements, not guaranteed incremental profit irrespective of physical gas prices. The model uses the actual covered prices and remaining unhedged exposure, not a perpetual $205m addback.\n\nThe current maintenance program is funded and cash remains positive. This supports a conditional business-quality pass to DCF despite weak current cash growth. It does not establish durable high growth: production is modeled flat centrally, and the stock must compensate for commodity, reserve and reinvestment uncertainty.\n\n## Environmental economics: three different items\n1. **AEC/environmental-attribute sales are operating revenue.** Nine-month revenue declined to $46.746m from $61.234m. Management expects approximately $50m of 2026 net Pennsylvania Tier 1 AEC sales. The model uses $60m gross revenue and approximately $10m related fees inside operating expense, giving $50m net—not an assumed 20% revenue increase.\n2. **The July $30m 45Z credit sale monetized previously recognized tax benefits.** The proceeds approximated carrying value, so there was no material additional income-tax benefit from the sale. It is removed from current normalized cash diagnostics, not blindly deducted from EBIT.\n3. **Future 45Z sales are a separate conditional opportunity.** Prepared remarks anticipate roughly $40m annually from 2027, while explicitly noting pending implementation rules. Central valuation allows $20m per year only in 2027–29; adverse allows zero and favorable $40m. No credit enters terminal value. Zero versus $40m changes value by only about $0.68/share, so the current WAIT does not depend on receiving the credits. Tax qualification, rulemaking and monetization remain risks.\n\n## Capital, reserves, perimeter and balance sheet\nThe October 29 remarks describe a maintenance-of-production program of about 1.5 rigs and a partial-year completion crew. Updated base capital is $563m, plus the first of three approximately $16m annual Utica-rights payments in 2026. **All base capital is retained** in the model; no supposed maintenance/growth split is used to inflate cash. The remaining 2027 and 2028 rights payments are also charged.\n\nApex was acquired in January 2025, so reported TTM comparisons are not perfectly equal-perimeter. Current capex includes $16.5m for Utica rights; the acquisition itself and disposal proceeds are not recurring FCF. The forecast assumes no additional acquisitions and no free growth from optional acreage. Separate favorable-case growth requires additional investment.\n\nYear-end 2025 proved reserves were **9.662 Tcfe**, including 6.972 developed and 2.690 undeveloped. The central ten-year forecast consumes 6.15 Tcfe before considering 2026 production. A 15-year flat-production continuation approaches the entire dated reserve base; perpetual value therefore requires conversion of additional resources and ongoing reserve replacement. Five-year development costs and the $2.221bn reported future development/abandonment schedule are not interchangeable with annual maintenance capex.\n\nPrincipal debt is $2,299.850m, versus $2,278.924m carrying value after discounts/fees. Finance leases are $33.245m. Only $1.977m of cash is unrestricted; restricted cash is not surplus. Asset-retirement obligations total **$189.043m**, including $21.075m current. The model reserves this existing claim once and does not add operating-lease liabilities when the corresponding expenses remain in operating cash flows. CNX and CNXM credit facilities have approximately $1.140bn and $533m unused capacity respectively, but are separate facilities, not freely interchangeable unrestricted cash.\n\nThe May 2026 convertible notes were settled; there is no residual convertible debt or duplicate conversion dilution. The share denominator is a 144.677225m proxy: October 14 common shares plus identified Q3 diluted award claims and anti-dilutive RSUs. This is not a complete future-grant roll-forward. Future SBC stays in operating expense, and there are no free assumed buybacks. A conservative $37m post-quarter buyback cash reserve matches the newer share-count basis without giving credit for undisclosed October cash generation; the exact dates do not align perfectly, a small disclosed bridge limitation.\n\n## Ten-year FCFF model\nThe valuation date is November 2. A **59-day residual 2026 stub** is followed by 2027–31 detailed operating years and 2032–36 transition years. Cash flows are discounted at 1 + 59/365 through 10 + 59/365 years. No elapsed October cash flow is counted again.\n\nCentral assumptions, all mapped by source and classification in `source_map.json`:\n- Production: 615 Bcfe annually, flat physical volume. Product mix is 562.5 Bcf gas, 8.6m barrels NGL and 0.15m barrels oil.\n- Henry Hub: $3.16 in 2027 from the EIA October outlook, then analyst midcycle $3.35/$3.50 in 2028–29 and 2% nominal escalation. Unhedged gas realizes `(Henry Hub + regional basis) × BTU conversion`; starting basis is −$0.70/MMBtu.\n- Covered gas: 409.4 Bcf at $3.30 in 2027, 233.1 at $3.20 in 2028, and 38.2 at $3.32 in 2029. These prices are already dollars per Mcf and are **not multiplied by BTU again**. No derivative balance-sheet asset is added on top of its modeled cash. Extra basis-only positions are disclosed as a sensitivity limitation, not a permanent benefit.\n- Initial NGL/oil prices: $25/$60 per barrel, then 2% inflation. Midstream, environmental and other revenue are separately identified.\n- LOE $90m, transport $395m, production tax 1.5% of physical sales, G&A $145m **including $25m economic SBC**, recurring other operating costs $68m, exploration $14m and other net corporate expense $14m. The fully burdened company cost metric is not used because it includes financing costs.\n- DD&A $550m is retained and scaled with activity/inflation. Base investment $563m is inflated and fully funded; finite rights payments and $5m annual affiliate investment are additional. Working capital consumes 3% of revenue growth.\n- Normalized tax is 25%, with only the explicit finite 45Z credit schedule. Tax-loss carryforwards are not assigned unverified permanent value.\n- WACC: 10% central, 11.5% adverse, 9% favorable. The framework uses verified October 30 Treasury 10-year 4.94% plus analyst equity-risk/beta and borrowing-cost assumptions, not an asserted market-consensus discount rate.\n\nTerminal nominal growth is 2% with no central real production growth. Net reinvestment is the greater of NOPAT × g / 10% incremental ROIC or the replacement-capital/affiliate/working-capital floor less DD&A. The model therefore does not erase depletion spending. Terminal value contributes 41.0% of central operating EV. Finite 10/15/20/25-year alternatives remain exhaustion approximations, not an engineered field-by-field reserve NAV.\n\n## Results and crosschecks\n| Scenario | Conditional value/share | Interpretation |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Adverse | $0.00 | Prolonged weak gas/cost stress exhausts modeled common equity; not a default forecast |\n| Central | $21.05 | Flat production, funded maintenance, expiring hedges, normalized taxes |\n| Favorable | $68.37 | Stronger gas/NGL economics, lower costs and funded growth |\n\nThe adverse raw calculation is negative and is floored at zero for common-equity limited liability. It is not a negative share price or an asset-liquidation valuation. Scenario outputs are neither probabilities nor guaranteed boundaries.\n\nCentral 2027 FCFF is $603.5m; operating EV is $5,603.3m and the equity bridge is $-2,557.2m. This is an operating-driver valuation, not an annualized-quarter cash yield.\n\n| Central-deck base capital | Value/share |\n|---|---:|\n| $525m | $22.80 |\n| $563m | $21.05 |\n| $600m | $19.35 |\n| $650m | $16.26 |\n\nFinite 15/20/25-year continuation values are $10.16, $13.59, $15.93. These show why reserve duration must not be hidden inside a favorable perpetuity.\n\n### Same-model reverse\nWith all other central inputs unchanged, the current price requires approximately **$0.531/MMBtu more in the unhedged gas deck**, scaled with inflation: Henry Hub about **$3.69 in 2027 and $4.05 in 2029**. Fixed covered hedge prices remain unchanged in this calculation. This is a plausible commodity outcome, not an obviously impossible expectation; it simply does not provide a conservative safety margin. No multiple-based shortcut replaces the FCFF reverse.\n\n### Peers\nThe historical, common-tax crosscheck uses AR and EXE, not CNX as its own peer. CNX trades at about 6.17× TTM EBITDA after exploration and SBC and 17.33× the historical tax-normalized FCFF proxy, versus AR upstream approximately 5.53×/16.62× and EXE 4.41×/12.65×. These are **not common-commodity-deck fair values**: integrated midstream exposure, acquisition timing, reserves, environmental credits and AR minority-history adjustments differ. AR’s AM stake is excluded consistently from upstream EV and operating cash; no double-counted AM dividend value. No peer median is applied as a target.\n\n## Margin, confidence and Stage 3.5\nThe current price is above the central conditional estimate; the model therefore supplies no positive margin of safety. For a commodity producer, the wide scenario range and reserve/hedge uncertainties argue for a larger discount, not a token one. A 25% discount to $21.05 is approximately $15.79 **arithmetically**, but it is **not an approved entry level**: the commodity deck, financial statements and technical trend must be refreshed at any future price.\n\nConfidence is moderate in the current **WAIT** decision and low-to-moderate in a single intrinsic-value number. The near-term contractual recovery is real evidence; it is not a perpetual growth franchise. A clearer margin could arise from a lower share price, durable improvement in unhedged realizations, lower verified sustaining costs, or better reserve conversion—not from changing the valuation method to force approval.\n\nThe technical review shows price above EMA200 and a weakly rising EMA200, with EMA50 slightly below EMA200; the long-term trend is recovering rather than unequivocally strong. This does not override the valuation conclusion. **Approved entry, stop, sizing and portfolio allocation remain null.**\n\n## Reproduction and limitations\n`history.py` reconstructs three annual periods, four recent quarters and TTM versus prior TTM from issuer filings; `constant_deck.py` separates price effects; `model.py` produces the three scenarios, ten-year schedules, finite-life sensitivity and same-model reverse. Source and independent QA files are retained in the supporting research record. This is a deterministic support model, not a banker-formula workbook certification.\n\nRemaining limitations: full regional hedge settlement calendar beyond the published aggregates; extra basis-only positions; exact future cash-tax/credit eligibility; full current award roll-forward; field-level reserve replacement; and post-quarter cash/share timing. None is silently treated as a guaranteed benefit. The result is a completed research-stage WAIT, not a completed purchase authorization.\n\nPrimary sources: [Q3 results and hedge/capital schedules](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1070412/000107041226000064/ex991supplementalq326.htm), [Q3 10-Q](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1070412/000107041226000066/cnx-20260930.htm), [FY2025 10-K](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1070412/000107041226000038/cnx-20251231.htm), [October 29 prepared remarks](https://investors.cnx.com/~/media/Files/C/CNX-Resources-IR-V2/events/2026/q3-2026-cnx-earnings-prepared-remarks.pdf). Full source-to-input mapping is in `source_map.json`.\n"},{"id":"A141","date":"2026-11-03","cat":"Selección y valoración","title":"ENTG — 3 noviembre: análisis completo; ESPERAR a $151.56","text":"PUBLICACIÓN DE INVESTIGACIÓN · 3 NOVIEMBRE 2026\nENTG: ESPERAR · WAIT_PRICE | Calidad: screen-grade | Decisión: WAIT_PRICE\nFecha del análisis: 2026-11-03. Precio de referencia: cierre regular del 2026-11-02, $151.56 USD.\nCurrent-price WAIT decision complete at $151.56. The conditional base value is $67.86 per share. The $50.89 research-review threshold is not an approved buy ceiling. No new entry is approved.\nSin nuevas compras autorizadas. Precio máximo de compra certificado: ninguno. Stop aprobado: ninguno. Acciones autorizadas: 0. La publicación no activa Stage 3.5.\n\n# Entegris (ENTG): full research record, no new entry\n\n**Decision as of the November 2, 2026 regular close: WAIT — valuation, not a failed business-quality gate.** Price $151.56; after-hours $152.50 is not the valuation reference. Conditional ten-year FCFF values are **$12.82 adverse / $67.86 base / $136.91 favorable**. None provides a margin of safety at the close. These are analyst scenarios, not probabilities, approved targets, or a guarantee that the market cannot justify a higher growth path.\n\n**Readiness:** historical cash/earnings gate completed; current-price rejection completed; valuation support is **screen-grade**, with explicit analyst drivers and a conservative diluted-share allowance. No Stage 3.5 entry, stop, size or trade is authorized. A more precise award roll-forward or maintenance split does not need to block the current decision: neither supplies the missing margin at $151.56. Those refinements matter before any future approval.\n\n## 1. Scope, prior work, dates and thesis\n\nPrior record A119 was a partial post-results review, not a full valuation. This work adds comparable earnings, the useful-life change, capital-payable reconciliation, a complete ten-year operating model, same-model reverse valuation, and limited homogeneous peer cross-checks. Latest results were released **October 29**, outside the user's November 2–6 earnings exclusion. **November 9 Investor/Capital Markets Day** remains a separate event risk; its future targets are not available evidence today. [S1–S3]\n\nEntegris supplies process materials and contamination-control products that become more important as semiconductor processes grow more complex. Recurring unit-related demand and customer qualification provide a stronger revenue foundation than pure equipment orders, but customer fab investment, utilization, advanced-node mix and export restrictions still make the business cyclical. The present case is operational improvement and monetization of installed capacity, not buybacks or a rerating assumption.\n\nThe principal positive surprise is real operating growth beyond accounting effects. The principal challenge is that the share price already capitalizes a long, strong expansion. Q3 Materials Solutions sales grew 15.9%; Advanced Purity Solutions grew 31.3%, with particularly strong segment profitability. Broad demand growth outside China supports the recovery, while China sales were flat. The stock's pullback is not, by itself, evidence of mispricing. No single issuer-specific adverse-news explanation for the whole pullback has been independently established here; it is not labeled harmless profit-taking. [S1–S3]\n\n## 2. Three fiscal years, four quarters and comparable TTM\n\nAll figures below are USD millions, consolidated GAAP unless indicated. Q4 2025 is FY2025 minus 9M2025; Q2 and Q3 cash-flow values are year-to-date differences. All eight four-quarter summation checks pass. Historical 2023/24 figures include businesses later divested and are not a clean organic growth chain. [S1, S4–S7]\n\n| Period | Revenue | EBIT | Net income | CFO | Cash capex | SBC |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| FY2023 | 3,523.9 | 499.2 | 180.7 | 644.5 | 456.8 | 61.4 |\n| FY2024 | 3,241.2 | 533.9 | 292.8 | 631.7 | 315.6 | 65.9 |\n| FY2025 | 3,196.6 | 455.9 | 235.6 | 695.4 | 299.2 | 69.3 |\n| Q42025 | 823.9 | 104.9 | 49.4 | 192.0 | 58.0 | 18.9 |\n| Q12026 | 811.9 | 141.6 | 92.0 | 183.0 | 41.5 | 16.7 |\n| Q22026 | 883.2 | 164.6 | 93.6 | 156.2 | 39.3 | 18.4 |\n| Q32026 | 1,005.7 | 230.6 | 157.8 | 266.8 | 43.2 | 19.7 |\n| TTM2025 | 3,222.5 | 501.0 | 288.5 | 679.5 | 348.7 | 66.0 |\n| TTM2026 | 3,524.7 | 641.7 | 392.8 | 798.0 | 182.0 | 73.7 |\n\n### Earnings normalization: real recovery, smaller than reported\n\n- TTM revenue rises **9.38%** to $3,524.7m. Reported EBIT increases from $501.0m to $641.7m, but $55.6m of 2026 depreciation savings comes from longer useful lives and $8.3m from an out-of-period tariff refund.\n- On the old useful-life basis, excluding the refund, TTM EBIT is **$577.8m**, or **15.33%** above $501.0m. Nine-month EBIT is $472.9m versus $351.0m, +34.7%; Q3 is $204.4m versus $122.6m. SBC remains an expense.\n- Refunds relate to costs incurred April 2025–February 2026. The exact quarterly expense allocation is not disclosed in the retrieved record. A deliberately conservative symmetric bound adds the full $8.3m to the prior TTM while removing the refund from the current TTM: **13.45% EBIT growth still survives**. Do not turn 15.33% into a falsely exact quality-band threshold.\n- Adding back all reported restructuring would give a TTM $23.5m current adjustment versus $21.3m prior. We do not assume repeated restructuring is economically free. The investment case passes without this addback.\n- Actual tax rates are unusually low and volatile. The forecast uses 20% base, 23% adverse and 18% favorable cash-tax assumptions, not the 9M 11.9% reported rate. Reduced interest expense from debt repayment helps net income but is not organic operating growth.\n\nThe useful-life change is material: manufacturing equipment moved from 5–10 years to 14, among other extensions. Q3 depreciation was reduced $17.9m; expected FY2026 reduction is approximately $73m. Historical comparable earnings subtract this benefit; corresponding old-life D&A is added back in the diagnostic FCFF bridge. The forecast instead uses an explicit physical depreciation/reinvestment basis, so **the life change is not charged twice**. [S2, S4]\n\n### Cash gate and capital-payable correction\n\n| Measure | Prior TTM | Current TTM |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| CFO | 679.5 | 798.0 |\n| Cash capex | 348.7 | 182.0 |\n| Accrual-adjusted physical investment | 330.8 | 190.1 |\n| SBC | 66.0 | 73.7 |\n| CFO minus accrual capex minus SBC minus current refund | 282.7 | 525.9 |\n\nThis last row is an **owner-cost cash diagnostic, not FCFF or distributable earnings**. It retains interest, actual tax payments and working-capital timing and does not establish permanent maintenance investment.\n\nNine-month cash capex fell $117.2m, but true investment fell less: current accrual capex = $124.0 + $25.3 ending equipment payable − $24.1 opening = **$125.2m**; prior = $241.2 + $17.2 − $57.6 = **$200.8m**. The apparent cash reduction partly reflects prior-year liquidation of capital payables. TTM accrual capex uses the equivalent September-to-September bridge. Government incentives are kept separate, not capitalized as recurring free cash. [S1, S2, S4]\n\nThe working-capital story is not a simple liquidation: 9M receivables and inventory consume $320.7m, versus $266.7m supplied by payables/accruals. Compensation and timing accruals can reverse. CFO improvements must therefore not be extrapolated from one strong quarter. The unlevered diagnostic using 20% normalized tax, matched old-life depreciation and actual accrual investment is $668.1m versus $454.2m before normalized working capital or replacement reserves; it is a cross-check, not the forecast starting FCF.\n\n**Gate verdict: passes to valuation.** Earnings growth survives accounting/tariff normalization, and cash is genuinely positive. Capex normalization is necessary but is not an automatic exclusion.\n\n## 3. Capacity, perimeter, asset quality and financing\n\nThe 2026 proxy shareholder letter states that the multiyear manufacturing investment cycle has concluded and the added capacity can support **more than $1bn of incremental revenue with limited further investment**. Relative to FY2025 revenue near $3.2bn, this supports a near-term capacity path around $4.2bn, not unlimited growth without capex. The base first forecast year reaches $4,229.6m. Beyond that point, explicit growth/replacement investment is retained. No primary maintenance-versus-growth capex split has been claimed. [S8]\n\nCMC Materials/Cabot-related acquired assets are not free: acquired book amortization is added back only alongside an explicit replacement-investment reserve and ongoing expensed R&D. The 2023/24 QED, electronic chemicals and pipeline/industrial-material divestitures mean those years cannot establish current organic growth unaided. The small industrial specialty chemicals sale in 2025 and Life Sciences Fluid Management wind-down/restructuring are noted rather than used to erase recurring costs. The latest 9M revenue bridge attributes $341.2m of increase primarily to volume and negative $13.1m to FX, supporting a demand-driven current comparison. [S1, S2]\n\nAt September 26: cash $356.5m, debt **principal** $3,295m, not the $3,260.6m accounting carrying amount. Principal maturities are zero through 2027, $400m in 2028, $2,000m in 2029 and $895m in 2030. The $750m revolver is undrawn and matures 2031. Nine-month principal repayments of $450m improve financial risk; they are not deducted again from FCFF. Leverage still magnifies the equity downside. Goodwill $3,950m and intangibles $769.1m require skepticism toward book-based returns. [S2]\n\nOperating leases remain operating costs; they are not added again to funded debt. The model reserves $150m of cash for operations (analyst assumption), giving net claims **$3,088.5m = $3,295 − $356.5 + $150**. It does not add present value of debt service on top of debt principal.\n\nOctober 26 outstanding shares were 152.8m; Q3 diluted weighted shares 153.9m. The model uses **155m shares**, a conservative existing-award allowance, not an exact November treasury-stock reconstruction. FY2025 outstanding options were about 1.3m at $89.66 and unvested RSUs about 1.3m; 2026 exercises/grants make that year-end schedule stale. No future buybacks are credited and forecast SBC remains expensed. At the proxy date, all directors/executives held 807,514 shares, less than 1%; BlackRock, T. Rowe Price and Vanguard were significant holders. These are dated ownership observations, not current trading signals. [S1, S2, S8]\n\n## 4. Ten-year FCFF construction and assumptions\n\nThe reproducible model is `model.py`; all annual outputs and source-basis assumptions are in `calculations.json`. It is a controlled support calculation for the research workflow, **not a banker formula workbook**. Figures are nominal USD; no currency conversion is needed. Year 1 is the rolling forward twelve months from the November 2 valuation date, calibrated to the latest reported September 26 TTM. It is not a literal September fiscal endpoint or an assertion that October actuals are available. Each annual cash flow is discounted at the end of its rolling forecast year; there is no separately modeled stub or double-counted FY2026 cash flow.\n\nForecast EBITA is after SBC, expensed R&D and physical depreciation, but before acquired intangible amortization. Finite acquired amortization is then deducted to display EBIT. Conservatively, cash tax is charged on EBITA: no uncertain acquired-book-amortization tax shield is credited. FCFF = EBIT − cash tax + physical depreciation + acquired amortization − physical capex − intangible replacement investment − incremental working capital. The 2% base replacement reserve is an analyst economic cost, not reported capex or a second SBC deduction.\n\n| Assumption | Adverse | Base | Favorable |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Growth years 1–5 | 12% / 6% / 4% / 4% / 3% | 20% / 14% / 12% / 10% / 8% | 25% / 20% / 17% / 14% / 11% |\n| EBITA margins years 1–5 | 23.5% / 24% / 24% / 24% / 24% | 26.5% / 28% / 29% / 30% / 30% | 28% / 30% / 32% / 33% / 33% |\n| Cash tax | 23% | 20% | 18% |\n| Physical depreciation/revenue | 6% | 5.5% | 5% |\n| Physical capex/revenue | 8.5% | 6.5% | 6% |\n| Intangible replacement/revenue | 2.5% | 2% | 1.5% |\n| Incremental WC/incremental sales | 25% | 20% | 15% |\n| WACC | 12.5% | 11% | 10% |\n| Terminal growth | 2% | 2.5% | 3% |\n| Terminal ROIC | 6.5% | 16% | 25% |\n\nThese are analyst paths, not consensus or management targets. Base next-year growth exceeds TTM growth but is supported directionally by Q3 acceleration and Q4 revenue guidance of $1,025–1,055m with GAAP EBIT of $235–250m. Base margin expansion requires higher utilization and better mix; it does not merely perpetuate the accounting life extension. Favorable growth and margins are deliberately demanding. Adverse combines a fading semiconductor upcycle, less efficient capital deployment, higher cash tax and financing risk.\n\nYears 6–10 fade growth linearly to terminal growth. Reinvestment/NOPAT transitions toward g/ROIC, **subject to a binding physical-capex/replacement/working-capital funding floor**; additional transition investment is separately shown and every detailed cash identity is checked. Terminal reinvestment is the larger of NOPAT×g/ROIC and that economic investment floor. There is no permanently free acquired asset replacement or unfinanced terminal growth. Adverse 6.5% terminal ROIC intentionally describes weak capital economics; base 16% and favorable 25% assume progressively stronger sustained returns.\n\nWACC is dated and judgmental: the Federal Reserve's November 2 release reports October 30 ten-year Treasury yield **4.94%**. An analyst beta 1.4 ×5% ERP yields11.94% cost of equity; 6% pretax debt cost and about12.4% debt weight give approximately 11.06%, rounded to **11%**. Beta, ERP and marginal debt cost are assumptions, not measured current market estimates. Scenarios use 12.5%/10%; historical bond coupons are not falsely labeled the current marginal borrowing rate. [S9]\n\n| Scenario | Equity value/share | PV terminal / EV |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Adverse | $12.82 | 39.6% |\n| Base | $67.86 | 51.0% |\n| Favorable | $136.91 | 58.7% |\n\nBase WACC10–12% and terminal growth 2–3% produces **$56.05–83.35**. Physical capex stress at 5%, 6.5%, 8% and 10% of revenue produces approximately **$70.41,$67.86,$62.02,$52.99** with all other base drivers held fixed. The modest benefit from low capex shows that recognizing completed capacity buildout does not, by itself, rescue the current price. The scenarios are not a statistical confidence interval; stronger growth/lower required returns than the favorable case can support higher values.\n\n### Same-model reverse valuation\n\nAt $151.56, changing only the first-five-year growth path requires approximately **33.4%,27.4%,25.4%,23.4%,21.4%** growth, versus base 20%, 14%, 12%, 10%, 8%. Alternatively, keeping growth fixed and shifting all explicit EBITA margins requires approximately **52.2%–55.7% EBITA margins**. All reinvestment floors, bridge, taxes, discounting and share assumptions remain unchanged. These are mathematical requirements, not predicted outcomes. Independent recalculation reproduces the market price in both cases.\n\n## 5. Comparable-company cross-check\n\nMKSI provides complementary semiconductor process technologies with acquisition debt; ONTO provides metrology/process control, with a different capital model and transaction perimeter. Neither is an exact pure-play peer. The following uses the **same November 2 closes and latest reported quarter annualized**, not TTM. EV uses basic shares plus debt minus cash, avoiding convertible if-converted dilution plus debt double counting. SBC remains expensed. EBITA adds acquired amortization; ENTG also removes the refund and life-change uplift. This is a consistency check, not a peer-average price target.\n\n| Company | Latest quarter | EV/sales annualized | EV/GAAP EBIT annualized | EV/owner-cost EBITA annualized |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| ENTG | Q3 2026 | 6.49x | 28.29x | 26.10x |\n| MKSI | Q2 2026 | 3.33x | 16.55x | 13.27x |\n| ONTO | Q2 2026 | 6.59x | 35.59x | 27.17x |\n\nENTG is materially richer than MKSI on these measures and near ONTO's EBITA multiple, so peers do not establish that the pullback created a bargain. ONTO's proposed Rigaku transaction and large financing cash balance make forward EV/perimeter uncertain; MKSI's convertible capital and quarter timing also limit precision. Reported peer debt carrying values and stale quarter-end shares make this a cross-check rather than a fully rolled capital-market valuation. No inference that ONTO or MKSI is an approved candidate follows. [S10–S13]\n\n## 6. Entry conditioning, confidence and concrete next decisions\n\n**No new money at $151.56 under this underwriting.** A provisional 25% discount to the base would be about **$50.89**, but this is merely a research-review threshold, not an approved entry ceiling. A move toward $50 would likely coincide with changed fundamentals and must trigger a full refresh rather than an automatic order. A price near the favorable value is not a margin of safety to the base.\n\nConfidence is **high in the reported data and need to normalize useful lives/capex payables; medium in the current no-margin decision; low-to-medium in exact ten-year fair value**. Cyclicality, finite capacity headroom, customer mix and terminal returns justify a larger rather than smaller cushion. A tighter ownership roll-forward, exact maintenance split or tax-amortization schedule will improve precision, but a much stronger sustained growth thesis is what would change the conclusion materially.\n\nNovember 9 questions: quantify remaining capacity by product and utilization; distinguish expansion, maintenance and compliance capex; explain growth investment after roughly $4.2bn sales; provide sustainable incremental margins after SBC; clarify Life Sciences exit costs, replacement R&D needs, working-capital accrual reversals and debt reduction priorities. A credible higher organic growth path with funded capacity and durable margins would trigger a new model, not a mechanical target increase.\n\nTechnical check: close 151.56 versus EMA20/50/200 of 150.128/148.292/135.649; EMA200 is rising; ATR 9.225 and average volume 3.057m. Intermediate/long trends pass. This does not cure valuation. No prospective trigger, structural stop, reward/risk qualification or portfolio size has been selected; funds and risk budget are unknown. If valuation and thesis later qualify, require a fresh signal and M/S record and size conditionally from risk budget and entry-to-stop distance, not from a fabricated allocation.\n\nThesis deterioration would be evidenced by order/design-win weakness, China restrictions spreading to other regions, repeated capacity spend without cash returns, sustained working-capital deterioration, or debt reduction stalling. Better accounting EPS alone would not upgrade the thesis. This publication records research only; no trade is authorized or executed.\n\n## 7. Reproducibility and QA\n\nRun `python history.py`, `python model.py`, and `python comps.py`. History includes eight quarter-to-TTM checks; model includes seven structural/cash checks. Independent reviewer supplied `independent_model_QA.py/.json` with 22 passing checks and `independent_QA_gate.json`. The initial transition presentation was corrected before delivery to fund every displayed capital driver and terminal replacement floor. No pending numerical QA failure remains. Supporting files include sources.json, calculations.json, history.json, comparables.json and run_log.json. This dossier is not represented as a fully certified buy approval.\n\n## Source ledger\n\n- **S1** [FY2025 10-K; FY2023–25 statements, perimeter, awards, amortization](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1101302/000110130226000012/entg-20251231.htm). Local `annual2025.html/.txt/.rows`.\n- **S2** [Q3 2026 10-Q filed October29; life change, debt, payables, balance](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1101302/000110130226000162/entg-20260926.htm). Local `q32026.html/.txt/.rows`.\n- **S3** [October29 earnings release and Q4 guidance](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1101302/000110130226000159/entgq32026ex991.htm). Local `releaseq32026.html/.txt/.rows`.\n- **S4** [Q3 2025 10-Q; prior nine-month comparisons](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1101302/000110130225000103/entg-20250927.htm). Local `q32025.html/.txt/.rows`.\n- **S5** [Q2 2026 10-Q](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1101302/000110130226000150/entg-20260627.htm). Local `q22026.html/.txt/.rows`.\n- **S6** [Q1 2026 10-Q](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1101302/000110130226000102/entg-20260328.htm). Local `q12026.html/.txt/.rows`.\n- **S7** [October29 issuer earnings slides; quarterly cross-check](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1101302/000110130226000159/entgq32026ex992.htm). Local `slidesq32026.html/.txt/.rows`.\n- **S8** [2026 proxy; shareholder letter and dated ownership](https://s205.q4cdn.com/144974603/files/doc_financials/2025/ar/Entegris-2026-Proxy-Statement.pdf).\n- **S9** [Federal Reserve H.15 November2 release, October30 yield](https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/h15/).\n- **S10** [MKSI Q2 release August5](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1049502/000119312526335274/mksi-ex99_1.htm).\n- **S11** [ONTO Q2 release August6](https://investors.ontoinnovation.com/news/news-details/2026/Onto-Innovation-Reports-2026-Second-Quarter-Results/default.aspx).\n- **S12** [MKSI November2 regular close](https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/mksi/history/).\n- **S13** [ONTO November2 regular close](https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/onto/history/).\n- **S14** [ENTG November2 regular close](https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/entg/history/).\n- **S15** [Official November9 Investor Day announcement](https://investor.entegris.com/news/news-details/2026/Entegris-to-Host-Investor-Day-on-November-9-2026/default.aspx).\n"},{"id":"A142","date":"2026-11-03","cat":"Entrada y seguimiento","title":"Lista de espera: prioridades HPE, SANM, CLS y HOOD; no valoraciones nuevas","text":"# Prioridades de revisión de la lista de espera\n\nNota de seguimiento realizada el 3 de noviembre de 2026 UTC, con cierres regulares del 2 de noviembre (16:00 EST, USD). Esta nota organiza el trabajo pendiente: no contiene nuevas valoraciones completas, techos de compra ni aprobaciones de entrada.\n\n## Alcance del inventario\nSe revisó la última ficha de cada ticker a lo largo de todo el historial. Las 187 fichas en los estados Esperar precio, En observación y Descartada no deben confundirse con las 82 candidatas del escáner del 2 de noviembre.\n\nAntes de incorporar los nuevos dossiers había 32 en Esperar precio, 128 en observación y 27 descartadas. Después de actualizar CNX y ENTG a Esperar precio, quedan 34, 126 y 27, respectivamente. VFC continúa pendiente por evidencia insuficiente. El recuento es exhaustivo de esas fichas; la comprobación de precios y eventos fue dirigida a las prioridades siguientes, no un análisis completo de las 187 empresas.\n\n## 1. HPE: revisión dirigida por precio y estructura financiera\nCierre del 2 de noviembre: 51,19 $. La ficha histórica A107 conserva un valor base de 51,02 $ y una referencia de revisión de 50 $; el precio está un 2,38 % por encima de esa referencia. Son cifras históricas que requieren revalidación, no valor razonable certificado actual ni permiso para comprar.\n\nRevisar por separado la deuda del negocio de financiación y la deuda operativa, la integración de Juniper y la caja. No se verificó un nuevo resultado financiero que ya obligue a reemplazar el modelo; los resultados de septiembre ya estaban recogidos. El anuncio del emisor del 2 de noviembre confirma resultados el 23 de noviembre, fuera de la semana excluida del 2 al 6.\n\n- [HPE: convocatoria oficial de resultados del 23 de noviembre](https://www.hpe.com/us/en/newsroom/press-release/2026/11/hpe-to-present-live-audio-webcast-of-fiscal-2026-fourth-quarter-earnings-conference-call.html)\n- [HPE: históricos de cotización](https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/hpe/history/)\n\n## 2. SANM: preparar ahora, actualizar después de resultados\nCierre del 2 de noviembre: 159,18 $, frente al precio de escáner histórico de 217,49 $ del 9 de octubre. El modelo A028 del 10 de octubre tenía base 154 $: el precio actual está un 3,36 % por encima de esa base antigua y un 26,81 % por debajo del precio de aquel escáner.\n\nLos resultados están convocados para el 9 de noviembre a las 17:00 ET, fuera de la semana excluida. Preparar las fuentes y las preguntas ahora; realizar la actualización final después de resultados para evitar rehacer el mismo trabajo. Los 154 $ no son una valoración actual certificada ni un umbral operativo de compra.\n\n- [Sanmina: convocatoria oficial de resultados](https://ir.sanmina.com/news/news-details/2026/SANMINA-CORPORATION-INVITES-YOU-TO-JOIN-ITS-FOURTH-QUARTER-AND-FISCAL-YEAR-2026-EARNINGS-CONFERENCE-CALL/default.aspx)\n- [SANM: históricos de cotización](https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/sanm/history/)\n\n## 3. CLS: nueva información posterior al modelo antiguo\nCierre del 2 de noviembre: 249,22 $, un 31,19 % por debajo del precio de escáner de 362,16 $ del 9 de octubre, pero todavía un 21,57 % por encima de la base antigua de 205 $ del análisis A028 del 10 de octubre.\n\nLos resultados del 26 de octubre y el Investor Day del 27 de octubre son posteriores a ese modelo. La publicación reporta ventas de 5.830 M$ (+83 %), margen operativo ajustado de 8,5 % frente a 7,6 %, y margen operativo GAAP de 7,9 % frente a 10,2 %. Hay que actualizar crecimiento, reinversión, participación de los accionistas y caja. La caída de precio no demuestra por sí sola que la acción esté barata ni habilita una entrada.\n\n- [Celestica: resultados presentados en la SEC](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1030894/000103089426000049/cls-20260930prcondensedfs.htm)\n- [Celestica: resultados e Investor Day](https://corporate.celestica.com/events/event-details/q3-2026-financial-results-2026-investor-and-analyst-day)\n- [CLS: históricos de cotización](https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/cls/history/)\n\n## Revisión secundaria: HOOD\nCierre del 2 de noviembre: 83,84 $, un 23,44 % por debajo del precio de 109,51 $ de la ficha del 7 de octubre, que no tenía valor base registrado. Los resultados del 27 de octubre muestran ventas +12 %, BPA GAAP −16 % y BPA ajustado −3 %.\n\nReabrir el análisis del deterioro de beneficios y su valoración antes de extraer una conclusión. No comprar solo porque haya bajado. En la biblioteca histórica revisada no se encontró una actualización sustantiva posterior a estos eventos para CLS o HOOD.\n\n- [Robinhood: resultados oficiales del tercer trimestre](https://investors.robinhood.com/news-releases/news-release-details/robinhood-reports-third-quarter-2026-results)\n- [HOOD: históricos de cotización](https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/hood/history/)\n\n## Separación respecto a los dossiers publicados\nVFC (A139) queda bloqueada por normalización esencial pendiente y no tiene DCF. CNX (A140) y ENTG (A141) tienen decisiones de esperar a precio actual basadas en modelos condicionales screen-grade. Ninguna de estas publicaciones autoriza nuevas compras. Las prioridades HPE, SANM, CLS y HOOD de esta nota no sustituyen sus fichas históricas ni crean valoraciones nuevas.\n"}].forEach(function(r){if(!seen.has(r.id)){window.FP_LIB.reports.push(r);seen.add(r.id);}});})();
